course: dictionary
lesson: 3937
title: Fuel: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fuel is a noun for something that is burned to make heat or power, like petrol, gas, coal or wood. «Coal, oil and natural gas are fossil fuels formed over millions of years from ancient plants and animals.» The words burned, heat and power tell you that fuels are things we burn.
explain: Fuel can also mean food, because food gives your body energy. «Breakfast literally breaks a fast: if you ate tea at 6 pm and play at 10 am, your body needs fuel before it can run.» Your body is like a machine that needs energy, so the clue is that fuel is what makes you run.
explain: Fuel can be a verb too. To fuel something means to give it fuel, or to make a feeling or situation stronger. Try the test: if you can put "to" in front and it still makes sense, check your meaning in the sentence. Ask yourself, "What is being made stronger here?"
fact: Fuel is something that is burned to make heat or power.
fact: Food is called fuel because it gives your body energy.
fact: Fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas took millions of years to form, and they will run out.
fact: Burning fossil fuels puts carbon dioxide into the air, which warms the planet.
fact: Fuels catch fire easily, so keep them away from flames.
exercise: «Coal, oil and natural gas are fossil fuels formed over millions of years.» In this sentence, what is a fuel? | A word for a fast car | A special shape of cloud | A kind of animal | *Something burned to make heat or power
exercise: «Breakfast literally breaks a fast: your body needs fuel before it can run.» What does fuel mean in this sentence? | *Food, which gives your body energy | A piece of wood for a bonfire only | A road sign | A kind of paint
exercise: Why are fossil fuels a problem for our planet? | *They will run out, and burning them puts carbon dioxide into the air | They make the air cleaner | They never run out, so we can use them forever | They are the same as solar power
test: Your kind words will fuel my hope. What does fuel mean as a verb here? | *To make the hope stronger | To pour water on | To tidy up | To be sad
test: «Solar and wind energy burn no fuel and give off no carbon dioxide while they work.» Which kinds of energy need no fuel? | Petrol and diesel | Coal and oil | *Solar and wind | Gas and wood
exam: What does it mean to fuel an argument? | To end it quickly | *To make it stronger | To tidy it up | To put out the flames
